messages:
samir.k: Hey team — noticed a weird typing regression in the JS SDK when using the edge bundle. Generic overloads that should return generic<T> are resolving to `any` in our sample app on node:edge. Repro steps below.

lauren.w: ugh. can you paste the minimal repro? is it the SDK types or the consumer tsconfig?

samir.k: Repro (quick):
```ts
// pkg: @redwood/sdk v2.1.4 (edge build)
import { createClient } from '@redwood/sdk';
const c = createClient();
// overload: request<T>(payload: Request<T>) => Promise<Response<T>>
async function foo() {
  const r = await c.request({ path: '/echo', body: { x: 1 } });
  // expected r.body to be typed as { x: number } but TS shows `any`
  r.body.x; // no autocomplete
}
```

samir.k: The same code in a CJS consumer (Node 18) gets proper typing. Only fails when consuming the ESM edge bundle (and when using `module: "esnext"` in tsconfig).

ajohnson: Interesting. could be our index.d.ts generation step for the edge build — maybe generic overloads are flattened by rollup/tsc plugin. Did you try `skipLibCheck` or `preserveSymlinks`?

samir.k: Tried `skipLibCheck: true` and also `skipDefaultLibCheck` — no change. The weird part is the emitted d.ts for the edge bundle *does* show the generic overload signatures, but TS still picks `any` when consuming.

mike-p: Does the consumer tsconfig have `moduleResolution: node16` vs `bundler`? Edge runtimes and ESM packages can trigger different resolution.

samir.k: Consumer uses `moduleResolution: node` and `module: esnext`. Swapping to `node16` made autocomplete come back. So seems related to resolution heuristic.

lauren.w: Ok that's actionable. We should update our sample app templates to recommend `moduleResolution: node16` for ESM + edge, or provide a `types` entry that points to a `types/node-compatible.d.ts` workaround.

ajohnson: Another theory: TS will fallback to the `.d.ts` for the package, but if the package's package.json has both `types` and `exports` with ambiguous entries, resolution can pick the wrong types path. Can you paste our package.json for the edge build?

samir.k: Here it is: 
```json
{
  "name": "@redwood/sdk-edge",
  "version": "2.1.4",
  "main": "dist/index.js",
  "module": "dist/index.mjs",
  "types": "dist/index.d.ts",
  "exports": {
    ".": { "import": "dist/index.mjs", "require": "dist/index.js" }
  }
}
```

ajohnson: That looks fine. But if a consumer uses `packageExports` resolution, TS might use the `import` path which points to a .mjs JS file that doesn't carry types info unless `types` mapping is also present in `exports`. Try adding `types` under exports: `"types": "dist/index.d.ts"` for the `import` entry.

mike-p: Yep, TS 4.9+ uses exports map heavily. Without a `types` subentry, it can’t find the right d.ts for the `import` entry and falls back. That would cause inference to degrade to `any`.

lauren.w: Quick fix plan: 1) Add a `types` field in the exports map for the edge package. 2) Update sample app tsconfig recommendation to `moduleResolution: node16` for ESM consumers. 3) Add a tiny test in CI that installs the packed tarball and checks that a TS consumer gets proper inference.

samir.k: I can open a PR with the package.json change and a sample-app tsconfig tweak. Want me to try the CI check too?

ajohnson: Please do. For the CI test, a tiny tsserver-based check or `tsc --noEmit` on a small fixture should catch regressions.

mike-p: Also file a small doc note in DevEx about why `moduleResolution` matters — we've seen this bite customers.

devex-bot: Created ticket DE-4123: "edge bundle types resolution -> generic overloads resolve to any in some tsconfigs" and assigned to @samir.k. :white_check_mark:

lauren.w: Thanks Samir. If you need a hand with the tarball CI harness ping me and I’ll pair. :raised_hands:

samir.k: Will do. I'll push a small repro branch and link it here in a bit.
